# external/larn — vendored NetBSD snapshot for the vect-hack Larn Module

This directory holds the NetBSD-maintained `games/larn` source, vendored
(not a submodule) for the vect-hack Larn Module. The NetBSD src tree is too
heavy to submodule wholesale, so only `games/larn/` (the engine proper) is
dropped here, pristine.

## Source

- Origin: NetBSD source tree, `src/games/larn/`.
- Fetched: 2026-08-06 from
  `https://cdn.netbsd.org/pub/NetBSD/NetBSD-current/src/games/larn/`
  (directory last-modified 30-Jun-2026 03:01 UTC on that mirror). The
  read-only data files (`datfiles/larn.help`, `datfiles/larnmaze`) were
  fetched the same day from the mirror's `datfiles/` subdirectory and are
  staged into `/lib` by `scripts/build/build-larn-wasm.sh`.
- Version: BSD Larn 12.0 (`-DVER=12 -DSUBVER=0`, per the NetBSD Makefile) —
  David Holland's 2008 modernization of the classic BSD Larn, kept compiling
  under modern toolchains as part of the NetBSD src tree. Per-file RCS tags
  are intact in each file's `__RCSID("$NetBSD: <file>,v <rev> ...$")` banner;
  the engine-wide version is what the Makefile pins via VER/SUBVER.
- License: Noah Morgan's original non-commercial grant permits use,
  modification, and ports of the source, provided that a profit is not made
  from Larn, its sources, or those activities. See `../../LICENSING.md`;
  `COPYRIGHT` in this directory records Morgan's copyright. The NetBSD
  modernization is by David Holland et al.

## Why this and not HunterZ/larn

The design spec (`docs/specs/2026-08-06-larn-module-design.md`
§ Source pinning) listed three candidates. The spike (Task 1) evaluated all
three on two axes — *compiles under Emscripten with cflags-only (no source
patches)* and *cleanest terminal interception surface*:

- HunterZ/larn 12.3 (`00d60428284f`) — K&R-era C with ~30 `static declaration
  follows non-static` conflicts (forward decls missing `static`) and bare
  `exit()` calls. The static/extern conflict is a hard error in clang C with
  no `-W` downgrade (only `-fpermissive` in C++ relaxes it), so it requires
  source patches — fails the cflags-only axis.
- HunterZ/larn 12.4-alpha2 (`e61f3fbfc31b`) — modernized headers, but
  `movem.c:218` has the same `static`/`extern` `screen` conflict, plus
  unconditional `#include <conio.h>` (MSDOS) and an unconditional curses
  back-end in `ansiterm.c`. Also needs source patches.
- This NetBSD snapshot — compiles cflags-only (22/22 .o, 0 errors, 0
  warnings) with the terminfo (termcap-successor) terminal surface, whose
  semantic-byte `lpbuf` protocol (ST_START / CL_LINE / CLEAR / CL_DOWN /
  CURSOR / BOLD / END_BOLD) is the same semantic-grid surface the design
  preferred from 12.3 — no JS ANSI parser needed.

Per the design's pick rule ("If every candidate needs source patches, vendor
the NetBSD-modernized snapshot"), this is the chosen source. See
`docs/specs/larn-spike.md` for the full surface contract and
`docs/DECISIONS.md` (2026-08-06) for the decision record.

## Pristine-source invariant

This tree is **never patched** — same rule as `external/NetHack` and
`external/angband`. All C compatibility lives in our tree
(`src/modules/larn/native/compat/`, reached via `-I` and `-include` in
`scripts/build/larn-wasm.mk`) and in `src/modules/larn/native/main-wasm.c`
(Task 6), both of which are ours. Do not edit files in this directory; if a
new NetBSD revision is vendored, re-fetch the whole directory from the
mirror.
